#!/bin/sh
# Bound to fzf's every(3) in bin/tmux-espanso-popup: emits an fzf action,
# reloading the picker's feed only when the espanso config actually changed
# since the last tick, not on every tick regardless.
#
# The gate matters for the same reason bin/tmux-obsidian-task-poll's does:
# an ungated every(3):reload would re-run the feed (an espanso + jq
# subprocess) and reset fzf's scroll/selection every three seconds even when
# nothing changed; this turns almost every tick into a cheap no-op instead.
#
# TMUX_ESPANSO_POLL_STAMP: path to a file this script owns, holding the
# last-seen change stamp. Falls back to a fixed /tmp path so a direct,
# manual invocation still behaves sanely; the popup sets its own
# per-invocation file so concurrent popups never share state.
#
# No `set -e`: the first tick's `cat "$stampfile"` legitimately fails (the
# stamp doesn't exist yet), and that's a normal case here, not an error to
# abort on -- same reasoning as bin/tmux-obsidian-task-poll.

stampfile="${TMUX_ESPANSO_POLL_STAMP:-/tmp/tmux-espanso-poll.stamp}"

# Never fight someone mid-keystroke in fzf's query box.
if [ "${FZF_IDLE_TIME_MS:-9999}" -lt 400 ]; then
  echo ignore
  exit 0
fi

config_dir=$(espanso path config 2>/dev/null) || config_dir=""
if [ -z "$config_dir" ] || [ ! -d "$config_dir" ]; then
  echo ignore
  exit 0
fi

# cksum, not stat: mtime is 1-second granularity, and cksum's own output
# includes each filename, so an added/removed/renamed file changes the
# stamp too, not just an edited one. -exec, not a `find | xargs` pipeline --
# espanso's own config dir lives under "Application Support" on macOS, and
# xargs word-splits that space, corrupting every path built from it.
stamp=$(find "$config_dir" -type f -exec cksum {} + 2>/dev/null | sort)
prev=$(cat "$stampfile" 2>/dev/null)

if [ "$stamp" = "$prev" ]; then
  echo ignore
  exit 0
fi

printf '%s\n' "$stamp" > "$stampfile"
echo "reload-sync($HOME/.dotfiles/bin/tmux-espanso-feed)"
